    "items": [
      {
        "q": "Explain how HNSW finds nearest neighbours.",
        "a": "HNSW builds a **multi-layer graph**. Every vector is in layer 0; a random, exponentially shrinking subset also sits in higher layers, so the top layers are sparse express lanes.\n\nSearch: start at an entry point in the top layer, **greedily move to whichever neighbour is closest to the query** until none is closer, drop a layer, repeat. In layer 0 keep a candidate list of size `ef_search` and return the best k.\n\nIt's approximate because greedy search can stop in a local optimum. Larger `ef_search` and `m` raise recall at the cost of latency and memory."
      },
      {
        "q": "What do m, ef_construction and ef_search control? How would you tune them?",
        "a": "- `m`: maximum links per node. Higher → better recall, more memory, slower build. pgvector default 16.\n- `ef_construction`: candidate list while inserting. Higher → better graph, slower build (default 64).\n- `ef_search`: candidate list at query time. Higher → recall up, latency up (default 40); must be ≥ k.\n\nTune by measuring: take ~1,000 queries, compute exact neighbours with a sequential scan, then plot recall@10 against p95 latency while raising `ef_search`. Pick the knee that meets your recall target; rebuild with a larger `m` only if `ef_search` alone can't get there."
      },
      {
        "q": "Pre-filtering vs post-filtering in vector search — what goes wrong with each?",
        "a": "**Post-filter**: run ANN for ~ef_search candidates, then apply `WHERE tenant = X`. With a selective filter most candidates are dropped and you return fewer than k results — sometimes none — although matches exist.\n\n**Pre-filter**: restrict to matching rows, then search. With an exact scan that's correct but slow; inside an HNSW graph, skipping non-matching nodes can disconnect the graph and hurt recall.\n\nFixes: **partition** by the filter (a per-tenant or per-session index), over-fetch, pgvector **iterative index scans** (keep scanning until k rows pass the filter), or engines with filter-aware traversal."
      },
      {
        "q": "HNSW or IVFFlat in pgvector?",
        "a": "**HNSW** by default: a better speed-recall trade-off, no training step, and inserts don't need a rebuild. Costs: slower build, more memory.\n\n**IVFFlat**: faster build and smaller, but it trains k-means centroids, so it needs representative data before the index is created; recall degrades as data drifts until you rebuild; and you tune `lists` (≈ rows / 1000 up to a million rows) and `probes`.\n\nAt large scale add quantisation (`halfvec`, binary) or a dedicated engine. Below a few hundred thousand vectors per partition an exact scan may be fast enough."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why one HNSW index per session instead of one shared table with a filter?",
        "a": "Every query is scoped to one session, so a shared index turns each search into a **selective filter on ANN** — the post-filter problem: top candidates from other sessions are discarded and recall drops.\n\nPer-session tables make the filter free (the index only holds that session) and keep each graph small. **Costs**: many tables and indexes, catalogue bloat, per-session build time, cleanup of expired sessions, no cross-session search. The alternative now is a shared table with pgvector's iterative scans or partitioning by session — say you'd benchmark both."
      },
      {
        "q": "Write the RRF formula. Why k = 60, and why rank-based?",
        "a": "`RRF(d) = Σ_i 1 / (k + rank_i(d))` over every retriever i that returned d, ranks starting at 1.\n\n- **Rank-based** because BM25 scores and cosine similarities live on different, query-dependent scales; normalising them is fragile, ranks are comparable.\n- **k = 60** is the original paper's robust default (Cormack et al., 2009): it flattens the gap between rank 1 and rank 2 so no single retriever dominates.\n- A document in only one list still scores 1/(60 + rank); one in both lists usually wins. Weighted RRF multiplies each term by a per-retriever weight."
      },
      {
        "q": "Explain BM25. What does it do better than TF-IDF?",
        "a": "Per query term: **IDF** (rare terms count more) × **term frequency with saturation**: `tf·(k1+1) / (tf + k1·(1 − b + b·len/avg_len))`.\n\n- `k1` (~1.2–2) saturates term frequency: the tenth occurrence adds little, unlike raw TF.\n- `b` (~0.75) normalises for document length, so long documents don't win just by containing more words.\n\nIt beats embeddings on exact tokens — IDs, error codes, names, jargon the embedder never saw."
      },
      {
        "q": "Bi-encoder vs cross-encoder — why not retrieve with the cross-encoder?",
        "a": "A **bi-encoder** embeds query and document separately. Document vectors are precomputed, so search is an ANN lookup over millions in milliseconds.\n\nA **cross-encoder** reads query and document **together** in one forward pass and outputs a relevance score. It sees word-level interactions and is far more accurate, but nothing can be precomputed: scoring a million documents means a million model calls per query.\n\nHence two stages: bi-encoder plus BM25 fetch 50–100 candidates, the cross-encoder reorders them, keep the top 5–10."
      },
      {
        "q": "On your data, hybrid search had a lower MRR than keyword search alone. What do you do?",
        "a": "Lower MRR means the right chunk is retrieved but **ranked lower**: fusion pulls in vector results that push the lexical hit down.\n\nOptions, each measured on the same set: a **cross-encoder reranker** after fusion (fixes the ordering directly); **weight** RRF toward BM25 for identifier-heavy queries; **route** by query type (IDs and codes → keyword, conceptual → hybrid); improve the vector side (better embedder, smaller chunks). Hybrid often wins recall at deeper k while losing MRR — exactly the gap a reranker exploits."
      },
      {
        "q": "When is reranking not worth it?",
        "a": "- Retrieval is already precise — the top 3 are nearly always right. Check MRR first.\n- A tight latency budget (autocomplete, voice) that 100–300 ms breaks.\n- k is small and the model reads every chunk anyway; reordering five chunks changes little.\n- Cost at very high QPS with a hosted reranker.\n\nDecide with numbers: the MRR or recall@5 gain against p95 latency and cost per 1,000 queries."
      },
      {
        "q": "pgvector or a dedicated vector database?",
        "a": "It depends on measured needs, not on doing RAG. pgvector wins when you already run Postgres, need transactions and joins with business data, and the index fits comfortably. A dedicated database wins when scale, heavily filtered search, high write rates or managed scaling outgrow Postgres. I'd benchmark both on our data: filtered recall@10, p95 under peak load, re-index time and monthly cost."
      },
      {
        "q": "When would you pick Elasticsearch or OpenSearch for RAG?",
        "a": "When keyword search, filtering and aggregations are central — logs, product catalogues, existing search infrastructure — and vectors are an addition. You get mature BM25 and operations the team may already know, with vector fields for hybrid search."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why isn't FAISS a database?",
        "a": "FAISS is an in-process library for fast similarity search over vectors in memory. It doesn't store data durably, handle concurrent updates, filter by metadata, replicate or serve over a network — you'd have to build all of that. Great for experiments and batch jobs, not as a production store on its own."
      }
    ]
